-- title:
--   Surjectivity of the place specialisation map of a fibre model
-- statement:
--   Fix a level $N \ge 1$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, a prime $\ell$, a field $k$ of characteristic $\ell$ and a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red} : A \to k$. Let $fm$ be a fibre model of level $N$ for these data: a pair of subrings $B_{\mathrm{fin}}, B_{\infty}$ of the base change $L_N :=$ `laurentBaseChange (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) (modularFunctionFieldFull N)` (the field generated over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ by the coefficientwise image of the $\mathbb{Q}$-field of divisor expansions inside $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$), each containing the constants from $A$ and containing respectively $\bar j$, $\bar j_N$ and $\bar j^{-1}$, each integral over the corresponding affine base ring generated by the constants and $\bar j$ (resp. $\bar j^{-1}$), together with ring homomorphisms $\pi_{\mathrm{fin}}, \pi_\infty$ to `modularFunctionFieldC k N` $= k(j, j_N)$ inside $k((q))$ which induce $\mathrm{red}$ on constants and send $\bar j, \bar j_N, \bar j^{-1}$ to `jqModC k`, `jqNModC k N` and its inverse, plus the remaining compatibilities of the structure. Assume $\mathrm{red}$ is surjective, assume given, for every divisor $d$ of $N$, modular polynomial data for level $d$ (a monic $\Phi \in \mathbb{Z}[x][y]$ of degree $\psi(d)$ annihilating the $q$-expansion pair), and assume that the level-$N$ polynomial $\Phi$, reduced modulo $\ell$ and regarded over $k(X)$, is separable. Then the induced specialisation map `fm.spPlace` from places of $L_N$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ to places of $k(j, j_N)$ over $k$ — where a place is a proper valuation subring containing the base field whose ring is a principal ideal ring — is surjective.
--
--   This is the going-down statement for the reduction of places along a fibre model: every place of the characteristic-$\ell$ modular function field arises from a place of the modular function field in characteristic zero. It is what makes the specialisation map a reduction defined on all places, and it is used in the results that specialise divisors and divisor classes, in particular the comparison of Hecke divisors and the computation of specialisations in $\mathrm{Pic}^0$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_CharPModel_FibreModel_spPlace_surjective.lean

